============================================================================
THE FEATURE, one paragraph
============================================================================
Neill has 3 projects tracked in the `projects` table: chief (this repo), jess (a
separate repo, ~/code-projects/personal-assist), and arch (repo_path is NULL on
purpose — Decision C: the fleet reads Arch's notes but must NEVER touch its code or
patient data). Today every job's isolation (`executor._make_clone` /
`_make_worktree`) always uses THIS repo (chief-command) regardless of which project a
job is actually for, and the builder's prompt never includes the target project's own
conventions or notes. Wire real per-project repo resolution through dispatch, the
builder, and the merge gate.

============================================================================
DESIGN DECISION — read this before writing any code, it resolves every backward-
compatibility question below
============================================================================
A job's `project_id` column is the switch:

  - project_id IS NULL/empty  → LEGACY PATH. Everything behaves EXACTLY as it does
    today: executor isolates against this repo (harness's own checkout) on branch
    "main" via the EXISTING defaults already baked into `_make_worktree`/`_make_clone`
    (their `HARNESS.parent` fallback); gatekeeper.merge() uses the module-level
    `REPO`/`MERGE_INTO` exactly as it does today. This is what makes every existing
    test in harness/tests/ keep passing unmodified — none of them ever set
    `jobs.project_id` today, so none of them are affected by anything in this task.

  - project_id IS SET (including "chief") → resolve the ACTUAL repo + default branch
    from the `projects` table (db/projects.py), for every project uniformly, no
    special-casing of "chief". If resolution fails, the job fails with a plain-English
    reason before any isolation/clone/worktree is attempted.

Going forward, `dispatch_local()` will ALWAYS stamp an explicit project_id (defaulting
to "chief" when the caller doesn't say) onto every NEWLY dispatched job — so this is a
real, live path for ordinary chief-command builds too, not just jess/arch. That's
intentional; it's what makes "which project" a first-class fact on every job from now
on, while every job/test that predates this feature (project_id NULL) is untouched.

